From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:09:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 33/60] perf(canvas): frame shells far from the view leave the
 render tree

Chrome gives every FrameShell it paints a compositor layer of its own, and
under the world's composited transform it paints a few thousand pixels
around the view. On a page of 182 desktop frames at 60%, about 6 frames are
in view, 96 shells are painted and the page has 134 layers. Every camera
frame rebuilds that layer list (PaintArtifactCompositor::Update) and the
compositor walks it, so a pan costs in proportion to the shells painted,
wherever they are.

A shell more than half a viewport from the view now gets the `hidden`
attribute (display:none), as tldraw culls the shapes outside its viewport.
The element stays, so nothing that reads data-frame-shell changes (wake
press, marquee, deep links, drag targets). FrameViews are never touched: a
selected, woken or streamed frame is drawn whatever the camera does, and a
culled shell still turns into one. A layout effect in Stage follows the
store's viewport in the same task as the camera write, in its own
subscription so apply() is unchanged, and a ResizeObserver follows the
stage. The in/out test is arithmetic over the frames' boxes (a turned frame
by its rotated bounds), and a camera frame in which no shell crosses a
margin writes nothing.

The margins, in viewports: a shell comes back at 0.5 (the previews' sharp
margin), goes at rest beyond 0.75, and goes while the camera moves only
beyond 1.5. Taking a shell out costs about 0.25 ms of style and layout.
Hiding at 0.75 during motion made the back-and-forth pan at fit 20-25%
slower (114 toggles); with the 1.5 margin it toggles nothing. Hiding only at
rest let a continuous 120-event wheel sweep at 60% grow to 29 shells in the
tree; with the drop margin it stays at 14.

localStorage['doop:cull'] = 'off' turns this off along with the rest of the
culling, and 'doop:cull-shells' = 'off' turns off this alone, for an A/B.

Rejected:
- visibility:hidden: needs an @layer override of Tailwind's [hidden] rule.
  It gives the same layer count at about 2x the style cost per toggle, and
  no difference in pictures at entry.
- content-visibility:hidden: 115 layers at 60%, against 40.
- Not rendering the shell: a React render per crossing, and the element is
  lost (its decoded picture, the wake press target), for the same layers.
- An inline display style: no different from the attribute.

Pictures at entry: the preview <img> is lazy, so under display:none it does
not load until the shell comes back at the 0.5 margin. With a deep link
plus 30 wheel events at 60% in a fresh profile, the on-screen shells with an
unloaded picture were the same with and without culling, all from local
preview failures. No shell was on screen while out of the tree. The browser
test pans at 2400 px/s into frames never seen at 25%. In every animation
frame it finds each on-screen shell in the tree with its picture loaded, and
frame 199's picture painted at the end.

Measured in headless Chromium with SwiftShader GPU compositing, 1600x1000,
on the 182-frame page. Wheel pan = 30 wheel events back and forth; long
pan = 30 one-way. Times are main / compositor thread ms, runs interleaved,
load 2.9-6.1.
Layers: 60% 134 -> 40, 28% 384 -> 116, fit (8%) 204 -> 198.
60% wheel pan: off 499/293 561/317 512/297 521/298;
               on  259/145 278/136 247/143 284/162 (2.0x / 2.1x)
60% long pan:  off 789/381 615/294 789/416 636/321;
               on  550/232 388/220 609/273 437/201
28% wheel pan: off 960/453 699/307 923/437 688/305;
               on  262/222 205/153 296/223 204/146 (3.4x / 2.0x)
28% long pan:  off 841/356 1447/299 815/339 1151/266;
               on  272/188 184/65 262/183 192/74
fit wheel pan: off 396/283 597/449 381/265 303/192;
               on  360/282 273/202 308/232 296/202 (no shell toggled)

